## How Long Do Variables Live?

A variable's **lifetime** is the range of code from its declaration to the end of its enclosing scope (the closest `{ }`).

### Basic Scope

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let x = 42;     // x's lifetime starts
  io.println(x);  // x is valid
  return 0;
  // x's lifetime ends — x is dropped
}
```

### Scope with Blocks

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let outer = 10;
  {
    let inner = 20;  // inner's lifetime starts
    io.println(outer);  // can access outer scope
    io.println(inner);  // can access inner scope
  }  // inner's lifetime ends — inner is dropped
  io.println(outer);  // outer still valid
  // io.println(inner);  // ERROR: inner out of scope
  return 0;
}
```

### Borrowing and Scope

```xiom
var data = Vec[Int]();
data.push(1);

{
  let m = &mut data;  // mutable borrow starts
  m.push(2);
}  // mutable borrow ends — data usable again

data.push(3);  // OK — borrow ended
```

### Drop Order

Variables are dropped in **reverse order of declaration** within a scope:

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let a = Vec[Int]();  // created first
  a.push(1);
  let b = Vec[Int]();  // created second
  b.push(2);
  return 0;
  // b dropped first (reverse order)
  // a dropped second
}
```

### Lifetimes and References

A reference cannot outlive the value it points to:

```xiom
// INVALID
// fn bad() -> &Int {
//   let x = 42;
//   return &x;  // x dies here, reference would dangle
// }
```

